## Eye-Popping Stats: Recalls Surge Past 400 This Year

With **433 food recalls** clocked in so far this year, we're well on our way to surpassing the all-time high. Of these, **105 are Class I recalls**, meaning they pose a serious health risk. For comparison, we're already 80% of the way to matching last year's total Class I recalls--and it's only May.

## Foreign Objects: A New Frontier in Food Safety?

This week's headlines were spearheaded by a slew of recalls due to foreign objects. Dakota Honey Company took center stage with **multiple recalls of honey products** tainted by stainless steel dust, plastic shreds, and who knows what else. Stainless steel dust in honey? Sounds like a culinary experiment gone horribly wrong, not something you'd expect to slather on your morning toast.

Adding to the mix, FINESALER LLC recalled their Alain MILLIAT Orange Marmalade due to **glass particles**. It seems clear jars are for display, not consumption.

## The Usual Suspects

On the allergen front, WE R NUTS and Ferris Coffee and Nut Company sent products packing due to mislabeling allergens. Meanwhile, Sugar Foods had the dubious honor of issuing a **Class I recall for Kroger Homestyle Croutons** after their supplier flagged potential Salmonella contamination in non-fat milk powder--a stark reminder of the interconnected risks in food supply chains.

## Repeat Offenders

Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC continue to lead the recall leaderboard, with each company racking up a damning 11 Class I offenses this year alone. With repeat offenders abounding, one has to wonder: Is corporate accountability truly being enforced?

## Conclusion

Foreign objects, allergens, and salmonella--not exactly the Holy Trinity of a balanced diet. As consumers, we put faith in these food companies, expecting them to keep us safe. But with 433 recalls and counting, that trust feels increasingly misplaced. It's high time for a systemic overhaul in food safety protocols. Until then, I'll stick to making my own honey--and you might want to consider doing the same.
